//! Hand-written Win32 declarations.
//!
//! This is the whole `unsafe` surface of Hanglock, and it is deliberately narrow: about forty
//! imports and a dozen structures, all of them stable since Windows 7. It is written by hand rather
//! than generated by the `windows` crate for one reason only — this build environment has no
//! crates.io access (`docs/decisions/0002-zero-dependencies.md`) — and adopting the generated
//! bindings later is a contained swap inside this crate.
//!
//! Two rules make hand-writing survivable:
//!
//! *   **Every layout is asserted at compile time.** `size_of` checks below turn a mistyped field
//!     into a build failure instead of memory corruption at runtime. Adding a field without the
//!     matching assertion is the one way this file can go quietly wrong, so the asserts sit next to
//!     the types they describe.
//! *   **Anything newer than the floor is loaded dynamically.** `SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext`
//!     and `GetDpiForWindow` do not exist on every supported Windows 10 build; a static import of
//!     them would make the executable fail to *start* there. Through `GetProcAddress` a missing
//!     export is a value the caller can test.

#![allow(dead_code, non_camel_case_types, non_snake_case, clippy::upper_case_acronyms)]

use std::ffi::c_void;
use std::mem::size_of;

